{:edb/db-before-t 1337 :edb/db-after-t 1338 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534650 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:15:08.998Z" 13194139534650 true] [17592186128921 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/766a7abf73e9f9ef85164b1165166d7493cd36cdc75d0f244e475fed8aeea8fd.png)\n\nRepresent the type I region above using set notation." 13194139534650 true] [17592186128921 1100 "Represent the type I region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/46d316127c71bdc5d921d5efb296b113.png)" 13194139534650 false] [17592186128921 1145 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x  \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x.$\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1.$\n\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1 \\leq x \\leq 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/acf870e74a9029543a6e6b21995c293f67f251b54dfad9584220780bc3a0b454.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=x+1$ and leaves through the upper function $y=7-x.$ So, we have $x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534650 true] [17592186128921 1145 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\quad g(x) \\leq y \\leq f(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x$.\n\n- The curve $x=y-1$ can be written as $y=x+1$.\n- The curve $y=7-x$ is already written as a function of $x$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n1 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x)$. A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/196068d46324da2557e27acfe2e6f5d5.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\ny=x+1\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function $y=7-x$. So, we have\n\n$$\nx+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x+1 \\leq y \\leq 7-x \\big\\}\n$$" 13194139534650 false] [17592186128922 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/3919a6a8442e983a9ca3465b2ed9abbeda18b7dc8d21b7c74b1851b53c1be714.png)\n\nRepresent the type II region above using set notation." 13194139534650 true] [17592186128922 1100 "Represent the type II region above using set notation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/527c08bc412d4f54db57900a3bb5d731.png)" 13194139534650 false] [17592186128922 1145 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq y  \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y.$\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}.$ Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2.$ So, we have $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/0cf258d0269db0977d5c19fe7abd159f13319bb493394fbd049e5660d88a1d6f.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{2-y}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}.\n$$" 13194139534650 true] [17592186128922 1145 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: a \\leq y \\leq b, \\quad g(y) \\leq x \\leq f(y) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y$.\n\n- The curve $y = 2-x^2$ can be written as $x = -\\sqrt{2-y}$. Note that we have picked the negative square root since $x\\leq 0$ in our region.\n- The curve $x=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}$ is already written as a function of $y$.\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=-2$ and $y=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y)$. A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/a1e788a35779364579390f7c39e5532d.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2-y}\n$$\n\nand leaves through the upper function\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD= \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: -\\sqrt{2-y} \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y-2}{2} \\bigg\\}\n$$" 13194139534650 false] [17592186128923 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534650 false] [17592186128923 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534650 true] [17592186128923 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\pm\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/59d32c44bae848d52adc188e15a8bab470b3eccd887cad45c231b66c486284fc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534650 true] [17592186128923 1145 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = \\pm\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/dc17a2cba3c4044e9c97228407b26379.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type I region.\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}$.\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534650 false] [17592186129102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301408 13194139534650 true] [17592186129102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301415 13194139534650 true] [17592186129102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301464 13194139534650 true] [17592186129103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301394 13194139534650 true] [17592186129103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301422 13194139534650 true] [17592186129103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301429 13194139534650 true] [17592186129103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301436 13194139534650 true] [17592186129103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301457 13194139534650 true] [17592186129104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301401 13194139534650 true] [17592186129104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301443 13194139534650 true] [17592186129104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301450 13194139534650 true] [17592186129104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301471 13194139534650 true] [17592186129774 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534650 true] [17592186129774 1145 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type I region has the property that a vertical line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type I region is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/d74059ee4370e106b54917ec43b007327805049033752867473d08da850001bf.png)" 13194139534650 true] [17592186129775 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534650 true] [17592186129775 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/438b119b6fd2c2f066705f9f44053faa766e3f57b6ee1a614794febf7a517a12.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139534650 true] [17592186129775 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1979/Source/Images/q-71816.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139534650 false] [17592186129775 1145 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{y-3}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $y=1$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/43b35101f6c57352975f3667ca76dece5782326c9af008990c8820a71bed504d.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=1$ and leaves through the upper function $y={x}^{2}+3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+3}.\n$$" 13194139534650 true] [17592186129779 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534650 true] [17592186129779 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles $x^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = 1$ and $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534650 false] [17592186129779 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circles ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139534650 true] [17592186129779 1145 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/24cbd9a0e01023dca6a289838d94efe3c7b67abe5b7a1f275f6945f280e65d64.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.75$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/6c51abe5c21571a9c5596fce4e3b8ace210eacfab125016e6b9a3b2a7bc2e056.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/f9fed5856eda128d84debec320bc38a35eaa2bf5184b1f81cb1194b02f8a306a.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301395 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1089 "q-168140" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1100 "Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1113 #uuid "2da77bc4-04e1-56ef-a7c8-2c62ea7852fc" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301394 1145 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nA type II region has the property that a horizontal line drawn through the region intersects the region in a single interval only.\n\nAmong the given options, the only type II region is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/1bee7870760a7c267ca83806f04b3fa8b933891fa5e5524ce76ee09f2c7c98e0.png)" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301396 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301397 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301398 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301399 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301400 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1041 "selection" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301399 13194139534650 true] [17592186301395 1110 #uuid "c0ce12bd-df4b-5c6f-9f35-6e39db5d376e" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301396 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/5ac45c6757e187796674ca4beda4ff73c60835b401df302bfd8a91fa5c98b68c.png" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534650 true] [17592186301396 1111 #uuid "8ae9c193-4220-55cf-b74e-6915341566b6" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301397 1034 "None of the listed" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534650 true] [17592186301397 1111 #uuid "0f78d1c2-aae9-567e-bd4b-a04931822ec1" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301398 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/36275eef6e634c83c614626bc2af6b97cd5bafddc8c02b64ba2a1aa7ea983317.png" 13194139534650 true] [17592186301398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534650 true] [17592186